.Dd October 3, 2026
.Dt MQ_OPEN 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm mq_open ,
.Nm mq_unlink ,
.Nm mq_timedsend ,
.Nm mq_timedreceive ,
.Nm mq_notify ,
.Nm mq_getsetattr
.Nd POSIX message queues
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In mqueue.h
.Ft mqd_t
.Fn mq_open "const char *name" "int oflag" "..."
.Ft int
.Fn mq_unlink "const char *name"
.Ft int
.Fn mq_timedsend "mqd_t mqdes" "const char *msg" "size_t len" "unsigned prio" "const struct timespec *abs"
.Ft ssize_t
.Fn mq_timedreceive "mqd_t mqdes" "char *msg" "size_t len" "unsigned *prio" "const struct timespec *abs"
.Ft int
.Fn mq_notify "mqd_t mqdes" "const struct sigevent *sevp"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
A queue has a name, which every process of the IPC namespace it was made
in shares, and messages of at most its message size, at most its count of them, kept highest priority first
and in the order they came within a priority.
The C library passes the name without its leading slash, and calls the
attribute calls through
.Fn mq_getsetattr .
A queue lives while its name or a descriptor has it.
.Pp
.Fn mq_open
returns a descriptor open on the queue
.Fa name
for reading, writing or both, with
.Dv O_NONBLOCK
and
.Dv O_CLOEXEC
as
.Fa oflag
gives them.
With
.Dv O_CREAT
it makes the queue if there is none, the caller's, with the mode its
umask leaves, and the count and size of the
.Vt struct mq_attr
given, at most 10 and 8192 for an unprivileged caller and 65536 and
16 MiB for a privileged one, or 10 and 8192 without the structure.
An unprivileged caller may make a queue while fewer than 256 exist,
in the caller's IPC namespace.
.Pp
.Fn mq_unlink
removes a queue name.
Opening requires search permission on the queue directory.
Creating or removing a name also requires write permission.
When the sticky bit is set, only the owner of the queue, the owner of
the directory or a privileged caller can remove the name.
.Pp
.Fn mq_timedsend
puts a message of priority
.Fa prio ,
below 32768, waiting while the queue is full until the absolute
real-time
.Fa abs ,
or not at all for a descriptor with
.Dv O_NONBLOCK .
The wait ends when real time reaches the deadline, between ticks, as
Linux's hrtimer ends it, or where the timer has no interrupt between
ticks at the first tick at which the clock has reached it.
A setting of the real-time clock or a leap second moves the deadline, as
in Linux: a step past it ends the wait at the next tick, and a step back
keeps the call waiting until the clock reaches it again; a step back after
the clock has reached it leaves the wait ended.
.Fn mq_timedreceive
takes the first message and its priority, waiting while the queue is
empty.
.Pp
.Fn mq_notify
registers the caller for the next message that reaches the queue empty
while no receiver waits:
.Dv SIGEV_SIGNAL
posts the signal asked for, with
.Dv SI_MESGQ ,
the value given, and the sender's pid and uid, unless the signal is 0 or
the process has run an exec since it registered;
.Dv SIGEV_NONE
posts nothing; and
.Dv SIGEV_THREAD
sends the 32 bytes at the value's pointer, with the last set to 1, on the
netlink socket whose descriptor is the signal, as musl's and glibc's
.Fn mq_notify
ask for it and read it in the thread they start.
Delivery removes the registration.
Closing any descriptor for the queue in the registered process also removes it.
A NULL
.Fa sevp
takes the caller's registration away.
A registration of
.Dv SIGEV_THREAD
that goes without a message sends its bytes with the last set to 2.
The kernel makes the datagram that carries them when the process
registers, as Linux does, so that a registration that succeeded is never
lost for want of memory.
Registering and taking a registration away set the queue's access and
change times.
.Pp
Each queue is charged to the real user of its maker, as on Linux: its
messages' bytes, and 12 longs for each message it may hold, up to 32768 of
them, for the kernel's bookkeeping.
The queues one user has made may hold at most its soft
.Dv RLIMIT_MSGQUEUE ,
819200 bytes by default, privileged or not, and those made in a user
namespace also count to its creator in the parent, up to the limit the
creator had when it made it.
The charge ends with the queue.
.Pp
.Dv FIBMAP
on a queue's descriptor is
.Er EPERM
without
.Dv CAP_SYS_RAWIO ,
and otherwise stores block 0 and fails with
.Er EINVAL ,
since a queue has no blocks.
.Pp
.Xr poll 2
reports a descriptor readable while the queue has a message and writable
while it has room, and a
.Xr read 2
of it gives Linux's line of the queued bytes and the registration:
.Dl QSIZE:N NOTIFY:N SIGNO:N NOTIFY_PID:N
.Pp
The
.Ql mqueue
file system lists the queues of the IPC namespace that mounted it as the
files of one directory, sticky and writable by all, mode 1777 and its
creator's when the namespace is made; every mount of a namespace shows the
same directory.
The kernel mounts one at boot at
.Pa /dev/mqueue ,
with
.Ql nosuid ,
.Ql nodev
and
.Ql noexec ,
for the first namespace, and
.Xr mount 2
mounts another with the type
.Ql mqueue ,
which a user namespace's owner may also do, as on Linux.
A queue file starts at 80 bytes.
Truncating it changes the size reported by stat.
Reads return the status line, and message traffic changes the queue's times.
.Xr chmod 2 ,
.Xr chown 2
and
.Xr utimensat 2
store on a queue's file or the directory what they set and the change
time, a
.Xr chown 2
that names no id among them.
Reading a queue's file
gives the line above, and
.Xr unlink 2
of it is
.Fn mq_unlink ,
with the same directory permission checks.
.Pp
A descriptor that
.Fn mq_open
returns is open on the queue's file, as on Linux:
.Xr fstat 2
of it reports what a
.Xr stat 2
of the file gives, with no link once the name is gone, and its link in
.Pa /proc/PID/fd
reads
.Ql / Ns Ar NAME ,
with
.Ql " (deleted)"
after the name once the name is gone.
The descriptor retains the private mount of its IPC namespace.
Opening a queue file with
.Xr open 2
retains the mount used for that open and supplies the same queue operations.
.Dv O_CREAT
creates a queue with the default limits.
.Dv O_PATH
only retains the inode and mount.
.Ss System call interface
On i386 the numbers are 277 to 282 in the order above, with the timeouts
as 32-bit timespecs, and 418
.Pq Fn mq_timedsend_time64
and 419
.Pq Fn mq_timedreceive_time64
with 64-bit seconds.
.Fn mq_getsetattr
copies the attributes out, the flags
.Dv O_NONBLOCK
or 0, then takes
.Dv O_NONBLOCK
alone from the new ones.
.Pp
On x86-64 an i386 program has the same calls, with i386's 32-bit
.Vt mq_attr
and
.Vt sigevent
and the high word of a 64-bit timeout's nanoseconds ignored, as Linux
does.
An x86-64 program's numbers are 240 to 245 in the same order, the timed
calls take timespecs of 64-bit fields, and there are no time64 forms.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn mq_open
returns a descriptor,
.Fn mq_timedreceive
the message's length, and the others 0; on failure -1 with
.Va errno
set.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EACCES
The name contains a slash, or the caller lacks the required permission
on the directory or queue.
.It Bq Er EPERM
The sticky directory forbids removal by this caller.
.It Bq Er EAGAIN
A full queue to send to or an empty one to receive from, with
.Dv O_NONBLOCK .
.It Bq Er EBADF
A descriptor not open on a queue, or not open for the transfer.
.It Bq Er EBUSY
Another process is registered.
.It Bq Er EEXIST
.Dv O_CREAT
and
.Dv O_EXCL
for a queue that exists.
.It Bq Er EINTR
A signal ended the wait, also one pending when the timeout ran out.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
An access mode of 3 on an existing queue, attributes outside the caller's limits, a priority
past 32767, a malformed timeout, a notification of another kind, a
signal past 64, a
.Dv SIGEV_THREAD
descriptor of a socket other than a netlink socket, or a flag besides
.Dv O_NONBLOCK
to set.
.It Bq Er EMFILE
The new queue would take its user past
.Dv RLIMIT_MSGQUEUE .
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
No memory for a
.Dv SIGEV_THREAD
registration's datagram.
.It Bq Er ENOTSOCK
A
.Dv SIGEV_THREAD
descriptor that is not a socket.
.It Bq Er EOVERFLOW
The new queue's bytes would not fit in a
.Vt long .
.It Bq Er EMSGSIZE
A message past the queue's size, or a buffer smaller than it.
.It Bq Er ENAMETOOLONG
A name past 255 bytes.
.It Bq Er ENOSPC
The namespace already contains 256 queues and the caller lacks privilege.
.It Bq Er ENOENT
No such queue and no
.Dv O_CREAT ,
or an empty name.
.It Bq Er ETIMEDOUT
The timeout ran out.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr msgget 2 ,
.Xr poll 2 ,
.Xr proc 5
